A||||9||WisconsinGT||Weird problem
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-15-99||12:19 AM||||Well, another day, another problem. Today I was driving to an appointment. I was behind a truck with gravel. So I went into another lane and crossed what I thought was a simple railroad track at 60mph. As I went over it, I found out that their was a bump. After 20 sec. the car stalled. I was costing in nutral and thought I would try to restart. It did and all was well. Until I hit 55. Then it quit again and this time it backfired. So now I am thinking that I am *#&$@%. I try and start it again. No problem. I then continue traveling at 45mph. Everything is going well. I get to my appointment and leave several hours later. I drive home (50 miles) and all is well. I even ran the car at 55mph. I am really confused. Would anyone have any thoughts on what the problem is? Many thanks.
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||07-15-99||02:30 AM||||That's a broken ignition module for sure. It cuts out when it heats up and the backfiring is because your car is not aware the engine is not running anymore so fuel is still being pumped to it. It combusts spontaneously because of the engine's heat.<p>My 86.5GT had the exact same problem and replacing the ignition module cured it completely.
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||07-15-99||12:20 PM||||Thanks a million Cliff. Is this a project the average joe (Like myself) can do? What do I ask for....Ignition module? Can you give me an idea on the cost? Cheers
Z||000003||PaUL||07-15-99||02:39 PM||||the ignitions module is very easy to replace. All you need is a screwdriver. Its in the distributor under the rotor.
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||07-15-99||04:23 PM||||Well, if <b>*I*</b> can do it, <b>anyone</b> can! ;)<br>
Z||000005||theogre||07-15-99||06:43 PM||||before spending money, make sure the fuel pump is operational.... Jarring the fuel tank is known to whack the fuel pump harness at times. the tank isn't in there all that solid. If the harness has been hit, it might not be making good contact, keeping the pump from running full pressure/volume.<p>The easier of the parts to fix is the dist module. But if that doesn't get it you should probably pull the tank down and check out the pump and harness.
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||07-16-99||12:10 AM||||Ah - but if the fuel pump wasn't working, it couldn't be backfiring.
Z||000007||theogre||07-16-99||02:12 PM||||probably not, but if it's intermitant, the screwy fuel pressure can have some odd results. It's worth checking to be safe. It's not very hard to check.
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||07-16-99||10:18 PM||||Thanks all for your help on this issue. I have been driving the car since with no problems. I do not know if the impact from the bump in the road messed up something for just that moment or not. Anyway, as I mentioned in one of my other post...I did the paper clip code check. I saw a 13 and a 44. So I am going to get a new Oxygen sensor and Ignition module. Is the Oxygen sensor a do-it-yourself progect? Have a great weekend.
Z||000009||JetroGT||07-16-99||10:29 PM||||The Oxygen sensor is very easy to replace. All you need is a wrench. I suggest waiting untill the engin is compleatly cool then spraying the old sensor on the Y-pipe with WD40 or some other penetrating oil to help loosen it. I replaced my sensor last month with a Bosch sensor that I bought from Track auto. "It was actually in stock!"
